                            {
                                "example_id": "4ca20687-8c25-46e8-b9ce-d7fa8b836a0f",
                                "sense_id": "4d920af1-f3a5-3cf2-8247-28419240df96",
                                "text": "Seward was not trusted by Greeley, Phillips, Lovejoy, and the radicals of their complexion. They apprehended that after using the \"American citizens of African descent,\" until he (Seward) should be safely seated in the Presidential chair, he would abandon the negro and \"Tylerize\" their Party; hence, they would not trust him"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "0e185dcc-eb31-4738-8f23-b750a2346d53",
                                "sense_id": "4d920af1-f3a5-3cf2-8247-28419240df96",
                                "text": "With the annual Presidential dinner to the Cabinet as a curtain-raiser, there is a short intermission—just one day—before the Diplomatic Reception, when it seems as if the White House becomes a setting for a Graustarkian novel, so bejeweled, besashed, beturbaned, and betiaraed are the guests."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "82227872-eeb9-4df0-950e-f129851d0894",
                                "sense_id": "4d920af1-f3a5-3cf2-8247-28419240df96",
                                "text": "When somebody points up a piece of statesmanship (as opposed to politicianship) in either man's record, there is always the rejoinder: \"He has been planning his Presidential climb for many years. This was part of the strategy.\""
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "f22c3d2e-a199-46e8-9616-edf4b84ef0e2",
                                "sense_id": "4d920af1-f3a5-3cf2-8247-28419240df96",
                                "text": "In the days before televised press conferences, or debates between Presidential contenders, the railroads carried the politicians to the people. Here, President Truman addresses a crowd from the verandah of his private car, at Sacramento during the 1952 campaign."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "bd6b5f37-18cb-4676-814f-156cec595a89",
                                "sense_id": "4d920af1-f3a5-3cf2-8247-28419240df96",
                                "text": "After delaying the decision for three years, the President eventually endorsed the concept in the foremath of the 1984 Presidential election, ..."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "08a18d99-442b-4a9c-b04a-af54e2de30a1",
                                "sense_id": "4d920af1-f3a5-3cf2-8247-28419240df96",
                                "text": "Is there any precedent in Ohio for a downballot candidate receiving on a percentage or absolute basis so many more votes than the Presidential candidate of the same party in this or any other presidential election?"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "dbc20797-b09e-4339-84bf-ca8327bf068f",
                                "sense_id": "4d920af1-f3a5-3cf2-8247-28419240df96",
                                "text": "Thursday night's \"Daily Show\" began with another thorough take-down of John McCain's stance on Don't Ask Don't Tell, but afterward Jon Stewart moved on to McCain's 2008 Presidential running mate Sarah Palin, who he dubbed \"America's Tweetheart.\"\nPalin has taken to Twitter to vent her frustrations with Obama's handling of the latest WikiLeaks document drop, but Stewart believes her outrage is primarily unfounded."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "cfcbc06a-285a-4156-8bf6-1f9bc190b9ca",
                                "sense_id": "4d920af1-f3a5-3cf2-8247-28419240df96",
                                "text": "Let me pose a question: What if President Biden were to exercise his exclusive constitutional authority and issue an explicit White House statement that “the United States does not now recognize, nor has the United States ever recognized, the sovereignty of China over the island of Taiwan and the Pescadores.[…]”[...]\nNot so far-fetched would be a “negative” Presidential declaration along the lines described above: “the United States does not now recognize, nor has the United States ever recognized, the sovereignty of China over the island of Taiwan and the Pescadores.”"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "5269fc73-4c7a-4bca-8916-03187725a729",
                                "sense_id": "4d920af1-f3a5-3cf2-8247-28419240df96",
                                "text": "On December 7, 2015, Donald Trump, then a dark-horse candidate for the Republican Presidential nomination, used his Facebook page to promote a press release."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [],
                "word_linkages": []
            }
        ],
